 "answer": "Based on the context provided, there is no specific mention of a Postgres function used to get the on-disk size of a pgvector index. The context mainly discusses how to set up and use the pgvector extension with different versions of PostgreSQL via Docker and other installation methods, but it does not detail specific functions related to querying metadata about the pgvector index itself.\n\nIn general, to determine the on-disk size of a table or index in PostgreSQL, you can use the `pg_total_relation_size()` function. This function returns the total disk space used by a table or index, including all its associated indexes and TOAST data. Here's how you might use it:\n\n```sql\nSELECT pg_total_relation_size('your_pgvector_index_name');\n```\n\nReplace `'your_pgvector_index_name'` with the actual name of your pgvector index. This command will give you the total size of the index on disk.\n\nIf you specifically need to focus on the pgvector index, ensure that you have the correct index name or table name that includes the pgvector column or vector data. Note that this approach is a general PostgreSQL method and is applicable to any index or table, including those that involve pgvector.",
